You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: path-transform

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "Empty path is stored VERBATIM as the empty string. It is NOT defaulted/normalized to \"/\", even when both scheme and host are set. path() prints an empty line.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"\"<END>, line7=\"http://example.com:80\"<END>, line8=1. Compare (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "If path is non-empty and its FIRST BYTE is not '/', exactly one '/' is prepended. This happens at construction time (it is visible in path(), line 4), not only in to_string(). Pseudocode: if (!path.empty() && path[0] != '/') path = \"/\" + path;",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/foo\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/foo\". (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"foo/bar\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/foo/bar\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The leading-slash insertion is unconditional on the first byte: it fires for ANY non-'/' first character, including space, TAB, CR, '.', '?', '#', '\\\\', and a letter followed by ':' (no scheme-relative or query/fragment special-casing).",
    "evidence": "\" /a\" -> line4=\"/ /a\" (od: `/ <sp> / a`); \"\\t/a\" -> line4=\"/\\t/a\"; \"?q\" -> line4=\"/?q\", line5=\"\" (query empty); \"#f\" -> line4=\"/#f\", line6=\"\"; \"../x\" -> line4=\"/../x\"; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"a:b/c\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/a:b/c\", line7=\"/a:b/c\"; \"\\rx\" -> line4=\"/\\rx\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The leading-slash insertion ignores scheme and host entirely — it applies with empty host, empty scheme, and non-hierarchical schemes, producing triple slashes in to_string().",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/foo\", line7=\"http:///foo\". (\"mailto\",\"\",\"-1\",\"user@example.com\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/user@example.com\", line7=\"mailto:///user@example.com\". (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/foo\", line7=\"/foo\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "A path already starting with '/' is stored byte-for-byte unchanged: no second slash added, no duplicate-slash collapsing, no trailing-slash addition or removal.",
    "evidence": "\"/foo\" -> \"/foo\"; \"/foo/\" -> \"/foo/\"; \"//\" -> \"//\" (line7=\"http://example.com:80//\"); \"///\" -> \"///\"; \"//a//b//\" -> \"//a//b//\"; \"/a//\" -> \"/a//\"; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"//foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"//foo\", line7=\"http:////foo\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "from_components does NOT apply normalize_path(). Dot segments ('.', '..') are stored completely verbatim — never resolved, collapsed, or removed, in any position (leading, middle, trailing).",
    "evidence": "\"/a/./b\"->\"/a/./b\"; \"/a/../b\"->\"/a/../b\"; \"/../a\"->\"/../a\"; \"/a/b/..\"->\"/a/b/..\"; \"/a/b/../..\"->\"/a/b/../..\"; \"/./\"->\"/./\"; \"/a/b/./../c\"->\"/a/b/./../c\"; \"/.\"->\"/.\"; \"/..\"->\"/..\"; \"/a/..\"->\"/a/..\"; \"/./.\"->\"/./.\"; \"/../..\"->\"/../..\"; \"/a/b/c/d/e/f/g/../../..\"->\"/a/b/c/d/e/f/g/../../..\" (each also reproduced verbatim in line7)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Relative dot inputs get only the slash prefix, no resolution: \".\" -> \"/.\", \"..\" -> \"/..\", \"././\" -> \"/././\".",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\".\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/.\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/.\"; \"..\" -> line4=\"/..\"; \"././\" -> line4=\"/././\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "Segments that merely look dot-like are untouched (no over-eager matching): \"/....\" and \"/a/..b/c\" pass through.",
    "evidence": "\"/....\" -> line4=\"/....\"; \"/a/..b/c\" -> line4=\"/a/..b/c\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No whitespace trimming or stripping anywhere in the path. Leading, trailing and interior spaces, TABs, CRs, LFs are preserved byte-for-byte.",
    "evidence": "\"/a \" -> line4=\"/a \"; \"/a b\" -> \"/a b\"; \" \" -> line4=\"/ \", line7=\"http://example.com:80/ \"; \"/a\\tb\" -> \"/a\\tb\"; od of (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/a\\rb\") = `/ a \\r b \\n` on line 4 and `... : 8 0 / a \\r b` in to_string; od of \"/a\\nb\" shows literal `\\n` inside the stored path (so the 8 logical fields print as 10 physical lines)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No percent-decoding. %XX escapes are stored verbatim, including %2F (which is NOT decoded to '/'), %20, %41, %00.",
    "evidence": "\"/a%20b\" -> line4=\"/a%20b\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/a%20b\"; \"/a%2Fb\" -> \"/a%2Fb\"; \"/%41\" -> \"/%41\"; \"/%00a\" -> \"/%00a\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No percent-ENCODING either: unsafe characters are never escaped on the way in or out. Space stays a raw space, '\\\\', '[', ']', DEL (0x7F) and even the invalid-UTF-8 byte 0xFF survive unchanged in both path() and to_string().",
    "evidence": "\"/a b\" -> \"/a b\" (not \"/a%20b\"); \"/a\\\\b\" -> \"/a\\\\b\"; \"/a[b]\" -> \"/a[b]\"; od of \"/a\\177b\" -> line4 bytes `/ a 177 b`; od of \"/a\\377b\" -> line4 bytes `/ a 377 b`, exit=0"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Malformed percent escapes are accepted and stored verbatim with no validation, error, or effect on is_valid().",
    "evidence": "\"/%zz\" -> line4=\"/%zz\", line8=1; \"/%\" -> \"/%\", line8=1; \"/%2\" -> \"/%2\", line8=1; \"/a%%20b\" -> \"/a%%20b\", line8=1"
  },
  {
    "rule": "'+' in the path is NOT converted to space (and space is not converted to '+').",
    "evidence": "\"/a+b\" -> line4=\"/a+b\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/a+b\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No splitting on embedded '?' or '#': from_components never re-parses the path, so an embedded query/fragment delimiter stays inside path() and query()/fragment() stay empty.",
    "evidence": "\"/a?b\" -> line4=\"/a?b\", line5=\"\", line6=\"\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/a?b\"; \"/a#b\" -> line4=\"/a#b\", line5=\"\", line6=\"\"; \"/a?b#c\" -> line4=\"/a?b#c\", line5=\"\", line6=\"\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/a?b#c\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "Other sub-delims and reserved characters (';', '&', '=', '@', ':') are stored verbatim with no interpretation.",
    "evidence": "\"/a;b\"->\"/a;b\"; \"/a&b\"->\"/a&b\"; \"/a=b\"->\"/a=b\"; \"/a@b\"->\"/a@b\"; \"/a:b\"->\"/a:b\" (all echoed verbatim in line7, line8=1)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No case folding of the path. (Contrast: scheme IS lowercased, host is not, path is not.)",
    "evidence": "(\"HTTP\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"80\",\"/ABC\",\"\",\"\") -> line1=\"http\", line2=\"EXAMPLE.COM\", line4=\"/ABC\", line7=\"http://EXAMPLE.COM:80/ABC\". Also \"/ABC/DeF\" -> \"/ABC/DeF\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "UTF-8 multibyte sequences pass through unchanged (no IDNA/punycode/percent-encoding of non-ASCII).",
    "evidence": "\"/héllo/日本\" -> line4=\"/héllo/日本\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/héllo/日本\"; \"/…/ünicode%C3%A9\" -> line4=\"/…/ünicode%C3%A9\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No length cap or truncation on the path.",
    "evidence": "path = \"/\" + 5000 'c' chars -> line4 length = 5001, line7 length = 5022 (= len(\"http://example.com:80\")=21 + 5001), line8=1, exit=0. A 500-'b' relative path -> line4 = \"/\" + 500 b's"
  },
  {
    "rule": "to_string() splices the STORED path in verbatim with no separator of its own, and synthesizes NO '/' when the path is empty — so an empty path with a query yields \"host:port?query\" (authority immediately followed by '?'). Layout: to_string = (scheme.empty() ? \"\" : scheme + \"://\" + host + (port >= 0 ? \":\" + itoa(port) : \"\")) + path + (query.empty() ? \"\" : \"?\" + query) + (fragment.empty() ? \"\" : \"#\" + fragment)",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"q=1\",\"frag\") -> line7=\"http://example.com:80?q=1#frag\"; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"frag\") -> \"http://example.com:80#frag\"; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"q=1\",\"frag\") -> \"http://example.com:80/p?q=1#frag\"; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"p\",\"q=1\",\"frag\") -> line4=\"/p\", line7=\"http://example.com:80/p?q=1#frag\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "When scheme is empty, to_string() drops the whole \"scheme://host[:port]\" prefix and the path becomes the start of the string (a path-only relative reference); the stored slash-prefix is what makes it start with '/'.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line2=\"example.com\", line3=\"80\", line4=\"/foo\", line7=\"/foo\"; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"-1\",\"foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/foo\", line7=\"/foo\"; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"//foo\",\"\",\"\") -> line7=\"//foo\"; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7=\"\" (empty)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "is_valid() is driven by emptiness of host and path only: is_valid == (!host.empty() || !path.empty()). Scheme, query and fragment do not make it valid. Because any non-empty path input is slash-prefixed and thus non-empty, supplying ANY non-empty path makes the URL valid even with empty scheme and host.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7=\"http://\", line8=0; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line8=1; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"x\",\"\",\"\") -> line4=\"/x\", line8=1; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"q=1\",\"\") -> line7=\"http://?q=1\", line8=0; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"f\") -> line7=\"http://#f\", line8=0; (\"http\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7=\"http://:0\", line8=0; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7=\"\", line8=1; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line8=1; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line8=0; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"q\",\"\") -> line7=\"?q\", line8=0"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Path content never affects is_valid(): every syntactically hostile path tested (spaces, control bytes, 0xFF, bad escapes, embedded ?/#, dot segments) still yielded is_valid()=1 and exit=0. Only emptiness matters.",
    "evidence": "\"/%zz\", \"/a b\", \"/a\\rb\", \"/a\\377b\", \"/a?b#c\", \"/../..\", \"///\", \"/%00a\" — all line8=1, exit=0, no stderr"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Exit code is always 0 and stderr always empty regardless of path content (including invalid UTF-8 and 5000-char paths); malformed paths are never rejected via process status.",
    "evidence": "All ~65 runs reported exit=0 with no STDERR line, including od runs on \"/a\\377b\" (exit=0) and the 5000-char run (exit=0, /tmp/e5 empty)"
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "A NUL byte inside the path cannot be tested through argv, so behavior on embedded '\\0' is unknown.",
  "I could not distinguish whether the '/' prefix is added inside from_components or inside a path setter it calls — only that it is observable in path() (line 4), i.e. it is stored, not applied lazily in to_string().",
  "Whether the class exposes a separate normalize_path() that could be called explicitly is not observable from this 8-line output; the only established fact is that from_components does NOT normalize.",
  "Paths beyond 5000 chars (e.g. >1 MB, or near ARG_MAX) were not tested, so I cannot rule out a very large cap.",
  "is_valid() appears to be exactly (!host.empty() || !path.empty()), but I only sampled schemes 'http', 'mailto', 'x' and empty; a pathological scheme might add another validity term that interacts with path.",
  "Because the slash prefix guarantees any non-empty input path is stored non-empty, I could not construct a case with non-empty input path but empty stored path to further isolate the is_valid() term."
]

Then write finalSpec: complete, unambiguous pseudocode for this dimension that a Rust implementer can follow with zero guesswork. Include the exact literal outputs for any case where the behavior is surprising.

